      "message": "Will things continue to work if an application passes in a NetworkRequest created by:\n\n    NetworkRequest r \u003d new NetworkRequest.Builder().setNetworkSpecifier(\"1\").build();\n\n? AFAIK that code will result in the NetworkRequest having a StringNetworkSpecifier of \"1\", and that request will never match this network.\n\n\nI don\u0027t currently see another way to do this except:\n\n1. Add a hack to StringNetworkSpecifier to specifically match TelephonyNetworkSpecifier objects.\n2. Change the semantics of StringNetworkSpecifier.equals() so that it does something like:\n    return equals(other) ||\n            (other instanceof NetworkSpecifier \u0026\u0026 specifier.equals(other.toString()));\n3. (Probably too hacky) Make TelephonyNetworkSpecifier subclass StringNetworkSpecifier.\n\nPersonally I\u0027d prefer #2 because it is the smallest application-visible change and it maintains the current semantics of NetworkSpecifiers, which only match if the class is the same.",
